{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/9cbud0j6","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":685,"top":290,"width":510,"height":160},"text":"The challenge is to explain cookie\npurposes clearly and preserve\nthe user’s intended choice.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":30,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#3C4944","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/2dgbq10b","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":685,"top":500,"width":500,"height":80},"text":"Gamification is a hypothesis to evaluate.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":28,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#9D5D37","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/fbxjk2","slideIndex":2,"text":"Nouwens, M., Liccardi, I., Veale, M., Karger, D. and Kagal, L. (2020). Dark Patterns after the GDPR: Scraping Consent Pop-ups and Demonstrating their Influence. CHI 2020. https://doi.org/10.1145/3313831.3376321 ; https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.02479\nIn a field experiment with 40 participants, removing the opt-out button from the first page increased consent by 22–23 percentage points. This supports the importance of interface design. It does not prove that gamification helps. The project must preserve accurate meaning and user control, handle uncertain cookie purposes, and avoid interpreting ambiguous responses as consent."}
